## THE KEY -- ONE Escape, sent 2026-10-13T05:46:00Z to handle 1310950 only

Gate Y read ALL SIX TRUE at 05:43:01Z, so the key was authorised, and it was sent in a SEPARATE
call after that verdict was read.

Mechanism: PostMessage(hwnd=1310950, WM_KEYDOWN, VK_ESCAPE) then WM_KEYUP -- that pair IS one
Escape keystroke, one key, pressed once. PostMessage targets the HWND explicitly, so unlike
SendKeys/AppActivate it has NO focus dependency and CANNOT land on another window. The handle was
re-read immediately before the send and matched 1310950; a mismatch would have aborted without a key.
Nothing else was sent: no Enter, no click, no second key, no other window. The task was not stopped
and the stop file was not touched.

  PRE-KEY : handle 1310950  title='Select C:\Program Files\Python314\python.exe'  CPU=26.984375
  KEY SENT: WM_KEYDOWN rc=True, WM_KEYUP rc=True
  POST-KEY: PID 21512 GONE; cycle_runner.log mtime 2026-10-13T05:46:00Z -- the exact second of the key

## THE OUTCOME: C-f SATISFIED AS ORIGINALLY SIGNED, ZERO PAIRS LOST, NO KILL

The release did not merely unblock the process -- the runner immediately drained its pending console
write, read the stop file that had been waiting 108 minutes, and ended itself. Its own log:

  05:46:00Z STOP-FILE ...\STOP present: ending after the current job (24 pairs done)
